## Why This Is Asked
The task tests a non-trivial DP where block value is a segment maximum, not sum. It requires precomputing segment maxima and careful reconstruction, exposing trade-offs between time and memory.

## Key Concepts
- DP on prefixes with exact block counts
- Transition uses max(A[j..i])
- Precompute M[j][i] in O(n^2)
- Reconstruction pointers for endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumKBlocks(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const M=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ let m=-Infinity; for(let j=i;j>=0;j--){ m=Math.max(m,A[j]); M[j][i]=m; } }
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const split=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=Math.min(k,i);t++){
      for(let j=1;j<=i;j++){
        const val=dp[j-1][t-1]+M[j-1][i-1];
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; split[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best:dp[n][k]};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can you optimize to reduce memory with rolling arrays?
- How does this extend when blocks must have minimum gap?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design DP over partitions with a non-linear segment value, including reconstruction of segments. Encourages using prefix sums for fast segment sums and careful indexing to enforce exactly k non-empty contiguous blocks. Also invites discussion of time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations.

## Key Concepts

- Prefix sums for quick segment sums
- DP over partitions with exact k blocks
- Reconstructing the chosen boundaries via a prev table

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPartition(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) P[i] = P[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>new Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = (P[i]-P[0])**2 - c, prev[i][1] = 0;
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let j=t-1;j<=i-1;j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + (P[i]-P[j])**2 - c;
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; prev[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // recover endpoints via backtracking
  return {best: dp[n][k], prev};
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you reconstruct the actual segment boundaries from prev?
- Could you optimize to O(nk) with advanced DP techniques or convex hull tricks?
- How would changes to c (per-segment cost) affect the optimal k if it’s not fixed?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft DP with interval constraints and reconstruction using palindromic substrings.

## Key Concepts
- Palindromic substring precomputation in O(n^2)
- DP over prefix length and exact count: dp[i][t]
- Transition considers ending a palindrome at i with start p
- Reconstruction requires backpointers to recover substrings

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPalSubstr(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const isPal = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(false));
  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
    for (let j = i; j < n; ++j) {
      if (s[i] === s[j] && (j - i < 2 || isPal[i+1][j-1])) isPal[i][j] = true;
    }
  }
  const NEG = -1e9;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-1][t]);
      if (t > 0) {
        for (let p = 0; p < i; p++) {
          if (isPal[p][i-1]) {
            const val = dp[p][t-1] + (i - p);
            if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction would trace par[] to extract substrings
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) space?
- How would you reconstruct the exact substrings efficiently without extra passes?

